haracter extraction pipeline merges them into 1.**\n   146→\n   147→This proves:\n   148→1. The underlying text analysis CAN distinguish these characters\n   149→2. The character extraction pipeline is making a WRONG DECISION somewhere\n   150→3. The decision point is NOT in cross-group resolution where all fixes have been applied\n   151→\n   152→### Hypotheses for Next Attempt\n   153→\n   154→1. **The merge may happen BEFORE cross-group resolution**: The epithet \"the mummer\" may be classified and merged at an earlier pipeline stage (e.g., initial entity grouping, NER, or single-group resolution)\n   155→\n   156→2. **The cross-group resolution code may not be in the execution path**: Despite 9 attempts of modifications, the code may be conditionally skipped\n   157→\n   158→3. **Need to trace the ACTUAL execution path**: Add comprehensive logging at EVERY decision point to identify exactly where the merge occurs\n   159→\n   160→### Recommended Next Approach\n   161→\n   162→**STOP modifying cross-group resolution code until we verify it's actually being called.**\n   163→\n   164→1. **Add extensive debug logging** to trace the complete character extraction pipeline:\n   165→   - Log when initial entities are extracted\n   166→   - Log when entities are classified (epithet vs proper name)\n   167→   - Log when single-group resolution runs\n   168→   - Log when cross-group resolution runs\n   169→   - Log the EXACT point where \"the mummer\" gets linked to Prospero\n   170→   - Log the final character list before output\n   171→\n   172→2. **Identify the EXACT line of code** where \"the mummer\" gets added to Prince Prospero's alias list\n   173→\n   174→3. **Consider a fundamentally different approach**:\n   175→   - Post-processing step to DETECT incorrectly merged characters\n   176→   - Use chapter summary (which is correct) to validate character extraction\n   177→   - Add a \"death relationship\" post-filter that splits characters if one dies confronting t